---
// Paper Fig. 2F: primary failure category per designer, inlined so its text
// follows the site theme. Numbers in the notes come from the paper's result macros.
import svg from "@/assets/figs/library-design-bench/category-stack.svg?raw";

const CATEGORIES = [
  {
    key: "coverage",
    name: "Coverage",
    text: "Nothing close to the needed capability exists; the fix is a new operation.",
  },
  {
    key: "correctness",
    name: "Correctness",
    text: "The capability exists but has a bug: wrong output on a legitimate input, a misleading error, or a path too slow for the problem.",
  },
  {
    key: "rigidity",
    name: "Rigidity",
    text: "It nearly fits but cannot be adapted: a hard-coded policy, a data model that cannot hold the value, a monolithic operation, or a scope that excludes the input.",
  },
  {
    key: "verbosity",
    name: "Verbosity",
    text: "It fits, but using it takes excess code: a verbose interface, unpackaged wiring, shape conversion, or repeated declarations.",
  },
  {
    key: "deliverability",
    name: "Deliverability",
    text: "A simpler path through the library exists, but the implementer never found it, did not recognize it, or missed a fact needed to use it.",
  },
];
---

<figure class="ldb-category not-prose">
  <div
    class="cs-chart"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Stacked bars of the primary failure category per designer, for failed tests and for excess code."
    set:html={svg}
  />
  <figcaption>
    <b>Most excess code traces back to the library.</b> Primary failure category
    per designer. <b>Failed tests</b> classifies why at least one test failed;
    <b>excess code</b> classifies why the solution was longer than the reference.
  </figcaption>
  <details class="cs-notes">
    <summary>Categories and method</summary>
    <p class="cs-group">
      Limited by the library: no path through the library as shipped does
      better.
    </p>
    <dl>
      {
        CATEGORIES.slice(0, 4).map(c => (
          <>
            <dt>
              <span class={`cs-swatch ${c.key}`} />
              {c.name}
            </dt>
            <dd>{c.text}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="cs-group">
      Library not fully exploited: a path through the library as shipped removes
      the code or fixes the failure.
    </p>
    <dl>
      {
        CATEGORIES.slice(4).map(c => (
          <>
            <dt>
              <span class={`cs-swatch ${c.key}`} />
              {c.name}
            </dt>
            <dd>{c.text}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      <b>How.</b> For each library from six mini-SWE-agent designers, we sampled
      three solutions that failed at least one test and wrote more code than the
      reference. A GPT-5.6 Luna auditor read the library, the implementer's trajectory,
      the tests, and the reference, then classified each symptom by the smallest
      library change that would have prevented it. The bars show the primary cause:
      810 classifications per symptom, pooled across designers.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Takeaway.</b> 82% of excess-code causes are limits of the library itself.
      Rigidity and Verbosity alone account for 64%, against 14% for missing capabilities.
      For failed tests, Deliverability leads at 43%, and over half of those are an
      incomplete contract: the implementer found the capability but not a precondition
      or default needed to use it.
    </p>
    <p class="cs-scope">
      Each solution was audited once by one model, without running the proposed
      path. Shares describe partially passing solutions, not every solution.
    </p>
  </details>
</figure>

<style is:global>
  .ldb-category {
    --cs-ink: var(--foreground);
    --cs-ink-soft: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 62%, transparent);
    --cs-rule: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 14%, transparent);

    margin: 2rem 0;
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  html[data-theme="dark"] .ldb-category {
    --cs-rigidity: #31487d;
    --cs-verbosity: #5d84c2;
  }
  /* Below ~560px the chart scrolls sideways instead of shrinking its text. */
  .cs-chart {
    max-width: 680px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .cs-chart svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 540px;
    height: auto;
  }
  /* Scoped copy of the rule matplotlib ships as a global <style>. */
  .cs-chart svg * {
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-linecap: butt;
  }

  .ldb-category figcaption {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    color: var(--cs-ink-soft);
  }
  .ldb-category figcaption b {
    color: var(--foreground);
  }

  .cs-notes {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-notes p,
  .cs-notes dl {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    color: var(--cs-ink-soft);
  }
  .cs-notes b {
    color: var(--foreground);
  }
  .cs-notes .cs-group {
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .cs-notes dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 6px 14px;
  }
  .cs-notes dt {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 8px;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--foreground);
  }
  .cs-notes dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cs-notes .cs-scope {
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .cs-swatch {
    flex: none;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    transform: translateY(1px);
  }
  .cs-swatch.coverage {
    background: #c2410c;
  }
  .cs-swatch.correctness {
    background: #e0a086;
  }
  .cs-swatch.rigidity {
    background: var(--cs-rigidity, #1b2a4a);
  }
  .cs-swatch.verbosity {
    background: var(--cs-verbosity, #4a6fa5);
  }
  .cs-swatch.deliverability {
    background: #a3a7ae;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .cs-notes dl {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 2px;
    }
    .cs-notes dd + dt {
      margin-top: 8px;
    }
  }
</style>
